    "content": "<p>Before the towers, before the malls, before the first dhow of the modern age pulled into Dubai&#39;s deep-water harbour, there was a simple idea: build a port so good that the world would come to it. Jebel Ali, opened in the 1970s, has been proving that idea ever since.</p><p>Today it is the largest port in the Middle East and the anchor of one of the region&#39;s most important free zones. Here is how this remarkable trade hub works, and where it is headed.</p><h2>A Port That Ranks Among the World&#39;s Best</h2><p>Jebel Ali, operated by Dubai-based DP World, handled 15.5 million TEUs (twenty-foot containers) in 2024, its highest annual volume since 2015. That placed it ninth in the 2025 Lloyd&#39;s List ranking of the world&#39;s top container ports, and it was the only Arab port in the top ten. Reports put its handling capacity at more than 22 million TEUs a year.</p><p>The port also set a single-month record, handling 1.4 million TEUs in July 2024. Behind the figures lies a simple strength: Jebel Ali connects Asia, Europe, Africa and the Gulf on some of the world&#39;s busiest trade routes.</p><h2>Jafza: The Port&#39;s Commercial Twin</h2><p>Next to the port sits the Jebel Ali Free Zone, known as Jafza. Recent reports describe it as home to around 12,000 companies across manufacturing, logistics, retail, healthcare, automotive and food. In the first four months of 2026 alone, Jafza was reported to have attracted AED 854 million in new investment.</p><p>Free zones like Jafza typically offer:</p><ul><li>Full foreign ownership&nbsp;of companies.</li><li>Customs-bonded warehousing&nbsp;and port-side logistics.</li><li>Flexible licences and facilities, from small units to large industrial plots.</li><li>Favourable tax treatment&nbsp;for qualifying activities, subject to current rules.</li></ul><h2>Sea, Air, Road and Rail: A Truly Multimodal Hub</h2><p>The real genius of the system lies in how its parts connect.</p><ol><li>Sea.&nbsp;The port itself, linked to major global shipping lines.</li><li>Air.&nbsp;The Dubai Logistics Corridor, a customs-bonded link between Jebel Ali and Al Maktoum International Airport, lets cargo move from ship to plane quickly.</li><li>Road.&nbsp;An extensive highway network across the UAE and into the wider Gulf.</li><li>Rail.&nbsp;Etihad Rail now links Jebel Ali and other ports with industrial hubs across the country, and the Jebel Ali Rail Terminal is planned to grow from 800,000 to 1.6 million TEUs of annual capacity, with up to eight daily train services.</li></ol><p>That combination matters. When one route is disrupted, goods can move by another.</p><h2>Resilience Tested</h2><p>Global trade is never calm for long. Reports from the first half of 2026 noted that disruption to Middle East trade flows weighed on activity at Jebel Ali, even as DP World&#39;s wider global business grew. In response, the group handled nearly 200,000 TEUs by regional overland truck and rail across two months. It is a practical demonstration of why diversified routes are valuable, and why continued investment in rail and road connections is a priority.</p><h2>Technology Is Reshaping the Docks</h2><p>Modern ports are as much about data as they are about cranes. Automation, digital customs platforms and artificial intelligence are helping to predict congestion, track containers and speed up clearance.</p><h2>Green Ports, Green Funding</h2><p>Sustainability is moving to the heart of the sector. DP World has raised funds through a $1.5 billion green sukuk, and the wider shift to cleaner energy and electrified equipment is under way.</p><h2>What It Means for Businesses</h2><ul><li>Exporters and importers&nbsp;gain a fast, flexible gateway to markets across the Middle East, Africa and Asia.</li><li>E-commerce and retail brands&nbsp;benefit from nearby warehousing that supports quick delivery.</li></ul><h2>What is Jebel Ali?</h2><p>Jebel Ali is Dubai&#39;s flagship port and the largest in the Middle East, operated by DP World. It sits beside the Jebel Ali Free Zone.</p><h2>What is the difference between Jebel Ali Port and Jafza?</h2><p>The port handles ships and containers, while Jafza is the free zone beside it, offering licences, warehousing and industrial space for companies that trade through the port.</p><h2>Can a foreign company own 100% of a business in Jafza?</h2><p>Yes. Free zones such as Jafza generally allow full foreign ownership. Confirm the current terms with the free zone authority.</p><h2>How does Etihad Rail help Jebel Ali?</h2><p>It connects the port with industrial hubs and other ports across the UAE, offering a rail alternative to trucking and boosting resilience.</p><h2>Build Your Business on the World&#39;s Trade Routes</h2><p>Visit<a href=\"https://www.apexchief.com\" class=\"text-[#f7413e] underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">&nbsp;</a><a href=\"https://www.apexchief.com\" class=\"text-[#f7413e] underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apex Chief</a>&nbsp;for clear, thoughtful reporting on trade, logistics and the economy across the UAE and the Gulf. Bookmark us, and return for your next read.</p><p>Figures are drawn from published reports and may change. Free zone terms vary. Last checked October 2026.</p>",
