    "content": "<p>For decades, sovereign wealth funds were known as patient owners of real estate, listed equities, and infrastructure. That image is changing fast. Across the Gulf and beyond, state-backed investors are moving capital into deep technology, with a particular focus on semiconductor manufacturing. The reason is simple: chips have become the foundation of the modern economy, and nations want a stake in how they are designed and built.</p>\n<p>What Is Deep Tech?</p>\n<p>Deep tech refers to ventures built on substantial scientific or engineering breakthroughs rather than simple software or consumer apps. Examples include advanced materials, quantum computing, robotics, biotechnology, and artificial intelligence hardware. These fields demand heavy research budgets and long development timelines, which makes them a natural fit for investors with a multi-decade horizon.</p>\n<h2>Why Semiconductors Sit at the Centre</h2>\n<p>Semiconductors power everything from smartphones and cars to data centres and defence systems. The recent surge in AI has pushed demand for advanced chips even higher, while supply chain disruptions and geopolitical tension have shown how fragile global production can be. For governments, secure access to chips is now seen as an economic and strategic priority, not just a commercial one.</p>\n<h2>Why Sovereign Funds Are Well Placed</h2>\n<p>Building a semiconductor fabrication plant, known as a fab, can cost billions of dollars and take years before generating returns. Few private investors can stomach that risk. Sovereign wealth funds, by contrast, offer:</p>\n<li>Scale: Enormous pools of capital able to fund large projects.</li>\n<li>Patience: A long-term mandate that tolerates slow payback periods.</li>\n<li>Strategic alignment: Support for national goals such as diversification away from hydrocarbons.</li>\n<li>Convening power: The ability to bring governments, manufacturers, and technology partners together.</li>\n<h2>The Gulf Playbook</h2>\n<p>Gulf investors have already shown their hand. Abu Dhabi's Mubadala has long been a major shareholder in the global chipmaker GlobalFoundries, an early sign of the region's interest in manufacturing. More recently, Abu Dhabi has created dedicated technology-focused investment vehicles aimed at AI and advanced computing, and Saudi Arabia's Public Investment Fund has been steadily expanding its technology exposure. Together, these moves show a shift from financial ownership toward building real industrial capability.</p>\n<p>Chips and AI go hand in hand. For more on the UAE's AI plans, read how the UAE is building global leadership in AI and fintech.</p>\n<h2>Opportunities and Risks</h2>\n<p>The upside is considerable: new industries, high-skilled jobs, technology transfer, and reduced dependence on imported components. Yet the risks are real. Semiconductor manufacturing is capital-intensive, technically complex, and exposed to export controls and shifting trade policy. Talent shortages can also slow progress, and competition from established chip powers is fierce. Success will depend on smart partnerships and realistic timelines.</p>\n<h2>What It Means for Global Markets</h2>\n<p>When sovereign funds commit billions to deep tech, the ripple effects are wide. Start-ups gain access to patient capital, established manufacturers gain new partners, and regional economies gain a path toward knowledge-based growth. Other investors often follow the lead of these funds, amplifying the impact.</p>\n<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>\n<p>The pivot toward deep tech and semiconductor fabrication marks a new chapter for sovereign wealth. Instead of simply owning assets, these funds are helping build the industries of the future. For founders, policymakers, and investors, understanding this shift is essential: the next decade of technological progress will be shaped as much by who funds the factories as by who writes the code.</p>",
