    "content": "<p>A decade ago, most start-ups in the Middle East and North Africa built products for a single home market. Today, the region's most ambitious fintech and logistics companies are crossing borders, hiring across time zones, and competing for customers from Casablanca to Riyadh. This expansion is reshaping commerce in the region, and it holds lessons for founders and investors everywhere.</p>\n<h2>Why Fintech and Logistics Are Growing Together</h2>\n<p>The two sectors feed each other. E-commerce needs reliable delivery and convenient payment, and neither works well without the other. As online shopping has grown, so has demand for digital wallets, buy-now-pay-later services, and last-mile delivery networks. Companies that master one side often find natural opportunities in the other, which is why partnerships and integrated offerings are becoming common.</p>\n<h2>What Is Driving Cross-Border Growth</h2>\n<p>Several forces are pushing scaleups to look beyond their home markets:</p>\n<li>A young, digital population: Smartphone adoption is high and consumers are comfortable paying and shopping online.</li>\n<li>Market limits: Many individual countries are too small to support a company's long-term ambitions alone.</li>\n<li>Capital availability: Regional venture funds, sovereign-backed investors, and international backers are willing to finance expansion.</li>\n<li>Supportive hubs: Financial centres in the UAE and Saudi Arabia offer licensing frameworks and a base for regional headquarters.</li>\n<h2>The Gulf as a Launchpad</h2>\n<p>The UAE and Saudi Arabia have become the primary destinations for scaleups seeking larger, higher-spending customer bases. Saudi Arabia's digital payments push and growing e-commerce sector make it especially attractive, while the UAE offers a mature startup ecosystem and strong connectivity. Many companies founded in Egypt, Jordan, or Lebanon now treat the Gulf as their main growth engine.</p>\n<p>The UAE is a great base for tech companies. Learn more in our story on how the UAE is leading in AI and fintech.</p>\n<h2>Navigating Regulation Market by Market</h2>\n<p>Expansion is rarely simple. Every country has its own financial licensing rules, data-protection requirements, customs procedures, and consumer-credit regulations. A payments firm may need separate approvals in each jurisdiction, while a logistics company must adapt to varied import rules and address systems. Successful scaleups invest early in compliance teams and local partnerships rather than treating regulation as an afterthought.</p>\n<h2>Lessons From Scaleups That Get It Right</h2>\n<li>1. Localise deeply: Payment preferences, language, and delivery expectations differ widely between markets.</li>\n<li>2. Partner before you build: Working with local banks, telecoms, or carriers can accelerate entry.</li>\n<li>3. Protect unit economics: Growth that loses money in every new country is not sustainable.</li>\n<li>4. Hire local leaders: Teams who understand the market make better decisions faster.</li>\n<h2>Challenges on the Road Ahead</h2>\n<p>Currency volatility, uneven infrastructure, competition from global giants, and a tighter funding climate all test regional scaleups. Founders must balance ambition with discipline, expanding only where they can build a defensible position. Cross-border data rules and cybersecurity expectations are also rising, adding cost and complexity.</p>\n<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>\n<p>The cross-border expansion of MENA fintech and logistics companies signals a maturing regional economy. Instead of staying local, the strongest players are building infrastructure that connects markets, people, and businesses. For entrepreneurs, investors, and policymakers, the message is clear: the next wave of MENA growth will be regional by design, and the companies that master compliance, localisation, and execution will lead it.</p>",
