    "content": "<p>On a February morning in 2021, a small spacecraft slipped into orbit around Mars, and an entire region held its breath. When the Hope Probe confirmed its arrival, the United Arab Emirates became one of only a handful of nations to have reached the Red Planet. It was a moment of quiet, unshakeable national pride, and it was only the beginning.</p><p>Here is the story of the UAE&#39;s space programme so far, and the missions that will carry it into the next decade.</p><h2>A Short History of Big Ambitions</h2><p>The UAE&#39;s space journey is remarkably young. The Mohammed bin Rashid Space Centre in Dubai grew out of earlier satellite work, and the UAE Space Agency was established in Abu Dhabi in 2014. Within a few years the country was building satellites, training astronauts and planning interplanetary missions.</p><ul><li>2018:&nbsp;KhalifaSat, the first satellite fully built by Emirati engineers.</li><li>2019:&nbsp;Hazza Al Mansoori becomes the first Emirati in space.</li><li>2021:&nbsp;The Hope Probe enters Mars orbit.</li><li>2023:&nbsp;Sultan Al Neyadi spends around six months aboard the International Space Station.</li><li>2025:&nbsp;MBZ-SAT, the country&#39;s most advanced imaging satellite, is launched.</li></ul><h2>The Hope Probe: Watching Mars Breathe</h2><p>Hope launched in July 2020 and has been in Mars orbit since February 2021. Its high orbit allows it to observe the Martian atmosphere across full daily and seasonal cycles, a first for any Mars mission. The data helps scientists understand weather and how the planet&#39;s atmosphere has been lost to space over time. The mission is shared openly with researchers around the world.</p><h2>The Rashid Rover: A Lesson in Resilience</h2><p>The first Rashid rover reached the Moon in 2023 aboard a partner&#39;s lander, but it was lost when that lander crashed. The setback did not slow the programme. Rashid Rover 2&nbsp;has been developed by the Mohammed bin Rashid Space Centre, a compact rover with a scientific payload, and is planned to travel on Firefly Aerospace&#39;s Blue Ghost 2 mission to explore the far side of the Moon. If it lands successfully, the UAE would join a very small group of nations to operate there. Launch dates in this field shift often, so check the latest announcements for the current schedule.</p><h2>Emiratis Among the Stars</h2><p>The UAE Astronaut Programme has already produced pioneering flights, and a new generation is in training, including Nora Al Matrooshi, the country&#39;s first woman astronaut. A major goal is to see an Emirati on the lunar surface in the coming decade.</p><p>The country is also building a role in humanity&#39;s return to the Moon, contributing the Emirates Airlock, a crew and science airlock module for NASA&#39;s Gateway lunar station. In exchange, an Emirati astronaut is expected to fly to Gateway in future.</p><h2>The MBR Explorer: A Journey to the Asteroid Belt</h2><p>The country&#39;s most complex mission yet is the Emirates Mission to the Asteroid Belt. The MBR Explorer spacecraft is targeted to launch in 2028 on a journey of around five billion kilometres, using gravity assists from Venus, Earth and Mars. It will visit seven asteroids and attempt to land on the last one. It is a mission designed to help scientists understand the origins of our solar system.</p><h2>Why Space Matters Back on Earth</h2><p>Space is not only about prestige. It builds a knowledge economy: engineers, scientists, data specialists and entrepreneurs. Satellite data supports climate monitoring, agriculture, urban planning and security. In May 2026, a plan of roughly AED 1 billion to strengthen the space sector was announced, signalling that the ambition continues to deepen.</p><h2>What is the Hope Probe?</h2><p>Hope is the UAE&#39;s Mars orbiter. It entered Mars orbit in February 2021 and studies the planet&#39;s atmosphere and weather.</p><h2>What happened to the first Rashid rover?</h2><p>It reached the Moon in 2023 but was lost when the lander carrying it crashed. A second rover, Rashid 2, has been built for a future mission.</p><h2>When will the MBR Explorer launch?</h2><p>It is targeted for launch in 2028, with asteroid encounters expected around the end of the decade.</p><h2>Who was the first Emirati in space?</h2><p>Hazza Al Mansoori, who flew to the International Space Station in 2019.</p><h2>Follow the Journey</h2><p>Visit<a href=\"https://www.apexchief.com\" class=\"text-[#f7413e] underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">&nbsp;</a><a href=\"https://www.apexchief.com\" class=\"text-[#f7413e] underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apex Chief</a>&nbsp;for well-crafted stories on science, technology and national achievement across the UAE and the Gulf. Bookmark us, and return for your next read.</p><p>Mission schedules change. Check official sources for the latest. Last checked October 2026.</p>",
