        "content": "1. Lead with respect before efficiency.&nbsp;In many cultures represented here, relationships come first and business follows. A few minutes of genuine conversation, a greeting in someone&#39;s language, or a coffee before the agenda builds a bank of goodwill that pays dividends later. 2. Understand how authority is viewed.&nbsp;Some team members expect clear direction from a senior leader, while others expect to challenge ideas openly. Neither is wrong. Skilled leaders make their own style explicit, and invite input in ways that feel safe, for example by asking for written thoughts before a meeting so quieter voices are heard. 3. Communicate clearly and check understanding.&nbsp;English is the common working language, yet it is a second or third language for many. Use plain words, avoid idioms and slang, and confirm key decisions in writing. Asking &quot;what is your understanding of the next step?&quot; works better than &quot;does that make sense?&quot;. 4. Make feedback culturally intelligent.&nbsp;Direct criticism in front of the team can be uncomfortable or even damaging in some cultures. Give honest feedback privately, focus on the work, and be specific. Meanwhile, recognise good work publicly and warmly. 5. Build shared norms.&nbsp;Do not leave the team&#39;s culture to chance. Agree together on how meetings run, how quickly messages should be answered, and how disagreements are handled. A team charter, written once and revisited often, turns diversity into a working advantage."
